WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.010
Shade the world's hot deserts on a
map, and a strange pattern appears.

2
00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:08.870
They are not at the equator,
where sunlight is most direct.

3
00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:12.630
Many sit in two bands, north and south.

4
00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:16.710
Above them, the atmosphere works
like a gigantic conveyor belt,

5
00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.210
carrying heat towards the poles.

6
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:22.730
So why do so many line up
near the same two lines?

7
00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:25.390
Are those places closest to the Sun?

8
00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:29.090
In this mission, you'll meet
the forces that drive the wind,

9
00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:32.390
and see how Earth's spin bends moving air.

10
00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:34.770
Then you'll model how air
moves around the planet,

11
00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:37.130
and follow the jet streams.

12
00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:43.150
Your mission: explain why so many hot deserts
lie near the same two lines of latitude.
